and lo everyone Perry here with another  Sundance 2019 interview I am sitting  with the team responsible for the  souvenir and we have the opportunity to  talk to them thanks to the Kia Telluride  so huge thanks to our great sponsor for  making this happen I have to do this  just to jump right in because a lot of  our viewers do not know what the  souvenir is just yet so would either of  you mind giving a brief synopsis it's a  portrait of a young woman who wants to  be a filmmaker who gets involved with a  very charismatic man and he is very  supportive of her ambitions to be a  director but also destroys her  confidence in some ways and I know that  this is semi autobiographical for you so  I was wondering what inspired you to  tell your story this particular way well  it took me many years actually to feel  ready to tell the story I think probably  30 years ago I started thinking about  making a film on the subject and then it  just takes a long time to develop these  things I'd write ideas and notebooks and  then it just felt like the right time to  do it out of curiosity when you first  jumped into it compared to what I saw  last night are there any drastic changes  that happens a lot of changes happen a  lot of changes is changing all the time  in fact when we're making it is there  any tailoring these characters to the  fine folks that you cast in this film  because I'll be honest like I can't  picture your role any other way with any  other person in it well I'm yeah I met  Tom early on in the casting process and  I didn't look any further and what was  it like for you to know that she saw you  as a perfect fit for that role which is  I probably will not be able to shake for  a while and we were just talking  actually about how I see a lot of  midnight movies but it's different to  see someone who is an outright you know  easy to label villain versus someone  that's destructive to another person but  in a very natural almost subtle kind of  way yeah  I've always been fascinated by how the  most insecure people a lot of effort and  time goes into cultivating a kind of  note of certainty in their voice so that  to other people they appear to have the  whole picture and that can really draw  people in and Anthony I think seems like  one of those people when you first meet  him he seems to have the whole picture  he seems to have certainty he's almost a  kind of fundamentalist about art but  it's hiding this black hole inside him  and and one of Joanna's huge strengths  as a creator is her is her mobility and  so you really feel that you're allowed  to to make something around and that you  know that you're a kind of co-creator in  it and so yeah and just an answer to  that question yet you really felt you  could and there was a constant not only  a dialogue but I felt sort of beyond  dialogue as a sense of complicity even  about the kind of where I was sitting in  relation to the you know cuz I I'm one  of the first things we did I I sort of  cameras over there I kind of angled my  background because that just kind of  felt right and then I was going is that  fear am i doing that cuz I'm just scared  and it's like the second day and and I  think you came over and you said I quite  like it it's just you're this shape  emanating smoke and and so I I don't  know that it was a real complicity  between us all and David the DOP it just  it just felt like we all knew what story  we were telling even though it was  developing and changing with that point  in mind it makes me wonder what you like  to go to set with in hand in terms of  prep storyboarding anything like that or  do you like to stay more fluid because  some of these shots are just so specific  where you know a shot of the two of you  talking that just lets them sit and  breathe in a scene yes yeah well I  really fluid is the word I don't like  coming to search with anything in a way  I mean of course I'm thinking  working all the time as we're shooting  were shooting in story order which  really helps that fluidity but I really  I don't want anything in my hands I  don't 100 page script to carry around  because I want to observe and and and  and use my instinct and and I've got all  the material in front of me so no prep  material at hand but do you go with like  a coffee or something like that  coffee always considering that what was  the extent of this shoot how much time  do you have to do something like this  because there's some primary locations  but it does feel like you guys are kind  of all over the place and that the scale  and scope of this it feels big to me  well it's interesting because there was  not there was no scope in the sense that  we were in one place we were in an  aircraft hangar which was the setting of  the film school but the apartment is  built inside and then some of the  locations outside all in one building I  mean there's actually we were in a tiny  little area which was bliss because it  means there's more time to work there's  more time to play does that make it a  challenge from a visual perspective  feeling like you're stuck in one place  on one stand stage or even with the  apartment and things like establishing  the geography of the apartment - I find  it really exciting actually the more the  more limits in a way the more creative  you can be is there any specific example  of a situation where you were limited in  some respect and something beautiful  came out of it that everybody can keep  an eye out for I think I it's time it's  so liberating to work with the director  who is not obsessed with close-ups Oh  seeing your eyes or you know there's  this kind of idea that the eyes carry  the emotion and of course we all know  what what people mean by that but but  there's so many other things to read  especially in a two-shot or a wide and I  think there was once or twice when you  were almost tempted to come in close and  we kind of ran out of time and  I'm sure you would have ended up using  the too short or the weight anyway  because it just it just it's it's it  it's such a more it's just such a  different way of watching a film when  when you just hold back from that and  you're seeing knees and shoulders and  instead of just you know this all the  time but body language is so important  and the choreography of a shot and and  yeah when you admit exactly when you  stand back you can see the bodies moving  within space and that's so interesting  and I find yes if I'm suddenly you know  at the end of the day and we got half an  hour left for a scene often that scene  you get the magic in it and yeah I can  definitely see that happening also the  this shots where you're sitting opposite  one another that's when the body  language is kind of vital to me rather  than the traditional cutting back and  forth but I obviously have to talk a  little bit about honor here because oh  my oh my for a first lead role in a  feature film this is clearly not easy  whatsoever so one what made you  confident in her ability to take this on  and then to what kind of burning  questions did she have for you before  you started shooting well I I mean I  there was just a moment when I met her  quite late in the casting process  it's almost wondering if I was gonna  find the right person and she was  talking about her experience it was just  spontaneous it was it was just a  conversation it wasn't the casting  session or anything but she was talking  about her experience as a 19 year old  she was 19 when we shot the film and  there was something in what she said  that made me think that's Julie and then  from that moment I have that little  moment of realization and then I don't  well I didn't waver I mean I didn't  doubt anything I felt like I had her and  then we go on that journey and I enjoyed  I enjoy seeing what's going to unfold so  we don't have any audition process and  she very bravely unlike with Tom didn't  see a single thing written down so she  didn't have so m